# Shape of secrets/services.yaml. This file is a template committed in
# plaintext — it documents the keys, never the values.
#
# The real file is created with:
#   sops secrets/services.yaml
# which opens $EDITOR and encrypts on save to the recipients in .sops.yaml
# (your laptop key + kodo's host key). Credentials therefore never transit
# a terminal, a chat window, or shell history.
#
# Required keys:
#
# restic:
#   repository: s3:https://<ACCOUNT_ID>.r2.cloudflarestorage.com/<BUCKET>
#   password: <generate: openssl rand -base64 32>
#   r2-env: |
#     AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID=<R2 Access Key ID>
#     AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=<R2 Secret Access Key>
#
# Notes:
#
#   repository — R2 speaks the S3 API. ACCOUNT_ID is the hex string shown in
#   the Cloudflare dashboard under R2; BUCKET is the bucket name. The
#   `s3:` prefix is what tells restic to use the S3 backend.
#
#   password — restic's encryption key. There is no recovery path: lose it
#   and every snapshot is permanently unreadable. Store a copy in a password
#   manager before the first backup runs, not after.
#
#   r2-env — sourced as an EnvironmentFile by systemd, so it must be
#   KEY=VALUE lines with no `export`, no quotes, and no trailing spaces.
